WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.000
DQR is a runtime for circuit-cut quantum jobs on HPC and quantum hardware.

00:06.000 --> 00:10.000
A 32-qubit circuit turns into 72 independent fragments.

00:10.000 --> 00:15.000
Each fragment is an immutable descriptor, so DQR never reads quantum code.

00:15.000 --> 00:20.000
A label hints where each fragment runs, and the router may override it.

00:20.000 --> 00:24.000
Dispatch runs in waves. Rank 0 sends each fragment to the next free worker,

00:24.000 --> 00:27.000
and each result lands in GPFS.

00:27.000 --> 00:35.000
The local QPU rejects two fragments. DQR will try once more, then move them to HPC without restarting.

00:35.000 --> 00:41.000
Polling never blocks, so new waves keep dispatching while earlier fragments are still running.

00:41.000 --> 00:48.000
With a single QPU slot, workers idle while the quantum path drains. Slot count is the key lever.

00:48.000 --> 00:57.000
DQR then recombines the 72 values into the full observable, consistent with the CPU reference.

00:57.000 --> 01:02.000
The same dispatcher can target another backend. Here, IBM Torino.

01:02.000 --> 01:08.000
On the cloud QPU, slot count sets the critical path. Three slots need five rounds and 91 seconds.

01:08.000 --> 01:12.000
Twenty slots need two rounds and about 60 seconds.

01:12.000 --> 01:18.200
DQR: fragments as the unit of scheduling for hybrid HPC and quantum computing.
